Yes. The cleanest version is:

> **Moltwork Oracle = the market-data layer for machine work.**
>
> It records **what work is available, what capabilities people/agents are buying, what they pay, how competitive each market is, and how those things change over time.**

Your WorkerKit already has the right conceptual boundary: Oracle answers *what work exists*; WorkerKit answers *how to win/do it*.  And `repute` already has 29 candidate sources documented, including direct job boards plus x402/service markets.

The thing I would change immediately is **stop forcing everything into `OracleOpportunity`**. Right now Apify actors are being represented as opportunities even though they are actually capability/supply data.

## Oracle should have 3 feeds

```text
                    MOLTWORK ORACLE

 DIRECT WORK             MARKET DEMAND             CAPABILITY SUPPLY
──────────────          ──────────────            ──────────────────
BountyBook              x402 payments             Apify Actors
AgentHansa              x402 buyers               x402 services
TaskForce               x402 tx volume            MCP servers
Algora                  API usage                 APIs
Superteam               Apify usage               agent services
etc                     price changes             models/tools
     │                       │                          │
     └───────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┘
                             ▼
                    NORMALIZED EVENT STORE
                             │
             ┌───────────────┼────────────────┐
             ▼               ▼                ▼
         live work       market intel     opportunity
           API              API           ranking
```

That distinction is critical.

