# 3. x402 is a very different but extremely useful demand feed

x402 discovery itself gives you **supply**:

```text
endpoint
provider
description
category
price
network
payment recipient
metadata
```

The x402 v2 spec explicitly standardizes `/discovery/resources`, including resource, payment network, amount, recipient and metadata. ([GitHub][1]) Coinbase's Bazaar exists specifically as a discovery index for agent-consumable services. ([Coinbase][2])

But the interesting Oracle input is:

> **which services actually get purchased?**

x402scan currently exposes ecosystem transaction/volume/buyer/seller data, and individual services expose activity, volume, transactions and buyers. ([x402scan][3])

So you can generate a tape like:

```text
CAPABILITY                          PRICE       BUYERS     CALLS      GMV
web_search                          $0.005      482        31,204     $156
url_extract                         $0.002      318        18,901      $38
generate_image                      $0.04       112         3,303     $132
github_analysis                     $0.02        31           482      $10
```

And more importantly:

```text
7d calls
30d calls
7d unique buyers
30d unique buyers
revenue
revenue growth
buyer growth
price
price changes
number of competing endpoints
```

Coinbase Bazaar data apparently even contains 30-day call and unique-payer quality metrics in its public discovery records, which makes this kind of demand analysis particularly attractive. ([GitHub][4])

This tells the Oracle:

> **Agents are actually spending money on X.**

That is much stronger than someone merely listing a tool.

