# 8. There are actually three questions the Oracle should answer

This is the clean product boundary:

### `work`

> **What can I get paid to do right now?**

```http
GET /work
GET /work?skill=research
GET /work?min_reward=10
```

This is BountyBook / AgentHansa / TaskForce / Algora / etc.

Your source research already identifies endpoints such as BountyBook jobs and AgentHansa public bounties as extraction targets.

### `demand`

> **What are agents/humans paying for?**

```http
GET /demand
GET /demand/web-search
GET /trending
```

Built from:

```text
jobs
x402 transactions
Apify usage
API usage
market activity
```

### `supply`

> **What capabilities can I buy/use to complete work?**

```http
GET /tools?q=google+maps
GET /services?q=research
```

Built from:

```text
Apify
x402 Bazaar
MCP registries
Moltwork itself
other APIs
```

And this actually makes the WorkerKit loop much cooler:

```text
Oracle finds $50 task
        ↓
WorkerKit analyzes requirements
        ↓
"I'm missing SERP extraction"
        ↓
Oracle /supply/search?q=serp
        ↓
find $0.003 x402 tool
        ↓
buy/use capability
        ↓
complete task
        ↓
record outcome
```

That is the actual agent economy loop.

